+## Due times: switching on and rolling back
+
+Release 1.6.0 gives every ticket a due time ([decision 9](decisions/0009-due-times.md)). The data file stays version 1;
+a ticket record gains one field, `due`.
+
+1. **Deploy 1.6.0 with `DUE_TIMES` unset.** On its first start it gives every ticket in the data file a `due` (its
+   `created` plus the response time of its `priority`) and saves the file at once. The API still answers as 1.4.0 does.
+   Check that every ticket in the file has a `due`.
+2. **Set `DUE_TIMES=on` and restart.** Tickets now show `due`, and `GET /tickets?overdue=true` works.
+3. **Roll back** by starting 1.4.0 on the same file: it reads version 1, ignores `due` and shows every ticket. It drops
+   `due` the next time it saves. When 1.6.0 starts on such a file it gives the tickets a due time again, from the
+   response times then in force, so a due time set under other hours can differ from the one before the rollback. Keep
+   a copy of the file from before the rollback if that matters.
+
+The response times are `DUE_HOURS_URGENT`, `DUE_HOURS_HIGH`, `DUE_HOURS_NORMAL` and `DUE_HOURS_LOW` (whole hours, 1 or
+more). Changing them affects only tickets created after the restart.
+
 ## Notifications
 
 A change that should tell someone something (a ticket received, a ticket resolved) enqueues a notification in the outbox
